<translate>
<!--T:1-->
== Sent ==
* <code>Create</code> with a <code>Note</code> when an item appears.
* <code>Update</code> when an item that was sent as <code>Create</code> changes.
* <code>Delete</code> when an item that was sent as <code>Create</code> goes away, for example when the page is deleted or the section removed.
* Nothing for items that were never sent.
* <code>Accept</code> of a <code>QuoteRequest</code>, with a <code>QuoteAuthorization</code> as its <code>result</code>.

<!--T:2-->
The <code>Note</code> carries text as plain HTML and a link to the item, never wikitext. For <code>category</code> the text is the page's first paragraph. For <code>section</code> it is the section itself, because a heading is often just a time and says little.

<!--T:3-->
A feed with no followers sends nothing. Delivery goes to a server's <code>sharedInbox</code> when it has one, so a server with many followers gets one request.

<!--T:6-->
== Notes ==
Each item is a <code>Note</code> at <code>&lt;actor id&gt;/notes/&lt;item&gt;</code>, which answers while the item exists and is a 404 once it does not. For <code>category</code> the item is the page id, and for <code>section</code> it is the item's heading path and number in unpadded base64url, which stays one path segment whatever the headings hold. <code>attributedTo</code> is the actor, <code>url</code> is the page, <code>published</code> is the page's first revision, or for a translation page when its translation was complete, and the note is addressed to the public. <code>contentMap</code> holds the <code>content</code> again under the page's language as a BCP 47 tag, such as <code>ko</code>, or <code>en</code> for a translation page, since Mastodon takes a post's language from its first key. The page's URL, in <code>url</code> and in the content's link, carries <code>utm_source=fediverse</code>, and for a language's feed, unless UniversalLanguageSelector picks the interface language from the browser (<code>$wgULSLanguageDetection</code>), <code>uselang</code> with that language, so that a reader with no account sees the wiki's interface in the language of the translation. A browser that opens the note's id is sent to its <code>url</code>, as one that opens an [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Accounts#Actor</tvar>|actor id]] is sent to the page. A page gives its <code>category</code> notes hashtags with <code><nowiki>{{FEDIFEEDSTAGS:OpenTofu|MediaWiki}}</nowiki></code>, with or without <code>#</code>, which a translation page sets for itself when it sits in a translation unit. Each is a <code>Hashtag</code> in <code>tag</code> and a link at the end of the content, marked as Mastodon marks one, to the wiki's search for the tag. A tag that Mastodon would not read as one, such as one with a space, is left out. An edit that changes the hashtags of a page that is an item sends an <code>Update</code> of its note, which then has <code>updated</code>, when that edit was made.

<!--T:14-->
A <code>section</code> note's content starts with the heading path in bold, such as <strong>19 October 14:10</strong>. The section follows with its subsections: paragraphs and lists, links to other sites, and subheadings as bold lines. A bare link, which the page numbers like [1], shows its URL instead, since the number says nothing outside the page and changes when links are added above it. Past 500 characters of text it is cut with an ellipsis, and a link to the page ends the note. The <code>url</code>, which the link uses too, is the permanent link to the revision the section appeared in, or to the one that last changed its text, with <code>utm_source=fediverse</code> and then that revision's anchor for the section. <code>published</code> is when the section appeared. An edit that changes what the note says, a revert included, sends an <code>Update</code> of the same note, which then has <code>updated</code>, when that edit was made. Moving the section, or editing only past the cut, sends nothing. The note has no <code>summary</code>, which Mastodon would show as a content warning. It answers while the page's current revision has the path as many times as the item's number. When a path occurs more than once, the item numbered n is the occurrence that appeared n-th, counting up the page when the page adds sections above old ones.

<!--T:13-->
A <code>category</code> item stays a note after its page leaves the category. A follower got the item as news that a page appeared in the category, and like an RSS item it should not vanish later. A page usually leaves a category because the categories were tidied or moved, which followers need not hear about. Pages a follower would not want, such as spam or mistakes, are usually deleted, and a deletion sends <code>Delete</code>. Hiding the note or sending <code>Delete</code> on removal would be permanent or would flip-flop, because a page added back later is not a new item.

<!--T:7-->
Only a feed whose page sets a [[<tvar name="1">Special:MyLanguage/Accounts#Display name</tvar>|display name]] publishes notes. Any other feed's actor still answers, with an empty outbox and no notes, so maintenance categories publish nothing.

<!--T:8-->
A page that is an item of such a <code>category</code> feed links its note from its HTML head with <code>&lt;link rel="alternate" type="application/activity+json"&gt;</code>, so pasting the page's URL into a Mastodon search finds the note. When several such feeds hold the page, the first of its categories is used. A translation page links its note in its language's feed.

<!--T:15-->
== Quotes ==
Anyone may quote a note, as [<tvar name="1">https://w3id.org/fep/044f</tvar> FEP-044f] describes. Each note has an <code>interactionPolicy</code> whose <code>canQuote</code> gives <code>automaticApproval</code> to the public, so servers such as Mastodon 4.5 and later let their users quote it.

<!--T:16-->
A <code>QuoteRequest</code> whose <code>object</code> is one of our notes is answered with an <code>Accept</code>, sent as the <code>Accept</code> of a <code>Follow</code> is. Its <code>result</code> is a <code>QuoteAuthorization</code> at <code>&lt;actor id&gt;/quote_authorizations/&lt;id&gt;</code>, which is <code>attributedTo</code> the actor and names the quoting post as <code>interactingObject</code> and the note as <code>interactionTarget</code>. The request and the quoting post must be on the requesting actor's server. A request for a note we do not have is dropped without an answer. The quoting post is not fetched, since any post may quote.

<!--T:17-->
An authorization answers until the quote is withdrawn. An <code>Undo</code> of the request, or a <code>Delete</code> of the quoting post or of its actor, removes it, and it is then a 404, as it is once the note is gone. No <code>Delete</code> of the authorization is sent, since the post it covers is gone or withdrawn already. Authorizations are kept in the <code>fedifeeds_quote</code> table.

<!--T:9-->
== Outbox ==
The outbox is an <code>OrderedCollection</code> of <code>Create</code> activities for the newest 20 items, newest first. An item is recorded when it appears, whether or not the feed has followers, and is ordered by that time.

<!--T:4-->
== Received ==
* <code>Follow</code> is accepted and stored against the feed it names. <code>Undo</code> of a <code>Follow</code> removes it.
* <code>Like</code> and <code>Announce</code> of one of our notes are stored against its item, once per actor, item and kind. A <code>Like</code> with <code>content</code> and an <code>EmojiReact</code> count as likes. <code>Undo</code> of one removes it. Those of anything else are accepted and dropped.
* Replies, meaning a <code>Create</code> whose <code>inReplyTo</code> is one of our notes, are accepted and dropped.
* <code>QuoteRequest</code> for one of our notes is authorized. <code>Undo</code> of it, or <code>Delete</code> of the quoting post, removes the authorization.
* A <code>Delete</code> for an actor that neither follows, quotes, likes nor shares anything is dropped before its signature is checked.
* <code>Update</code> of an actor whose details are cached, carrying the actor's own document, refreshes them. Any other <code>Update</code> is dropped, and one from an actor whose details are not cached is dropped before its signature is checked.
* A <code>Delete</code> of the actor itself, given by its id or as a <code>Tombstone</code> with that id, removes what the actor left: its follows of every feed, its quote authorizations, its likes and shares, its cached details and its cached key. A job does it after the answer. An account that deleted itself no longer serves its key, so when the signature does not verify, the job first fetches the actor and goes ahead only if that answers 404 or 410.

<!--T:18-->
A stored like or share keeps the feed, the item, the actor's id and the activity's id, so that each actor counts once and an <code>Undo</code> or <code>Delete</code> finds it. An <code>Undo</code> is matched by the undone activity's id, or else by its kind and object. A page with any item shows on its <code>action=info</code>, such as <code>https://wiki.example.org/index.php?title=Post&action=info</code>, how many times the notes of all its items were announced and liked. After each total come the ten accounts that gave it most recently, linked to their profiles, then how many others did, then a link to <code>Special:FediFeedsReactions/Post</code>. That page lists every like and share of the page's notes, 50 at a time, grouped by note: a <code>section</code> note under its heading path, a <code>category</code> note under its category. Each row has the kind, the account and the time, and the newest come first.

<!--T:19-->
To name an account, the wiki caches the details of each actor that likes or shares: its id, its handle as <code>preferredUsername@host</code>, its display name, its profile URL (its <code>url</code>, or else its id), and when these were read. They come from the actor document that was fetched to check the signature. When there is none, or the details are more than a week old, a job reads the document. Every account whose details have been read is named, as other servers name the accounts that liked or shared a post, since a like or a share is public. One whose details have not been read yet is counted and named once they are. No avatars are shown. A <code>Delete</code> of the actor removes its likes, its shares and its details.

<!--T:5-->
Every other request is verified by HTTP signature. Remote keys are cached, because without a cache each request holds a PHP worker while it fetches a key from another server.

<!--T:12-->
Each actor's inbox and the shared inbox take the same activities. They answer 202 to what they accept and to what they drop alike, and 401 when the signature does not verify or the activity's <code>actor</code> is not the signer, except to a <code>Delete</code> of the actor itself. An accepted <code>Follow</code> or <code>QuoteRequest</code> is answered with an <code>Accept</code> sent to the sender's shared inbox, or else its inbox. The <code>followers</code> collection shows only <code>totalItems</code>, so who follows a feed stays private.

<!--T:20-->
== Turning interactions off ==
Likes, shares and quotes are taken only when <code>$wgFediFeedsInteractions</code> is true. It is false by default, so a wiki can publish feeds before its privacy policy covers the accounts that interact with notes. While it is false:
* <code>Like</code>, <code>EmojiReact</code> and <code>Announce</code> are answered with 202 and dropped. Nothing about them or their actors is stored, and no actor is fetched for them.
* Notes have no <code>interactionPolicy</code>, which Mastodon reads as nobody but the author may quote. A <code>QuoteRequest</code> for one of our notes is answered with a <code>Reject</code>, so the quoting server does not leave the quote pending, and nothing is stored.
* <code>action=info</code> shows no reactions, and <code>Special:FediFeedsReactions</code> only says that reactions are not recorded.
* Follows, deliveries, notes, the outbox and the feeds work as they do when it is true. An <code>Undo</code> or <code>Delete</code> still removes a like, a share or a quote authorization stored while it was true.

<!--T:10-->
== Signatures ==
Requests are signed and verified as Mastodon does, with [<tvar name="1">https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/draft-cavage-http-signatures-12</tvar> draft-cavage-http-signatures-12]:
* The algorithm is <code>rsa-sha256</code>; <code>hs2019</code> is accepted as the same.
* The signed headers include <code>(request-target)</code>, <code>host</code> and <code>date</code>, and for a <code>POST</code> also <code>digest</code>, which must match the SHA-256 of the body.
* A <code>Date</code> more than an hour ahead or twelve hours behind is refused.
* The <code>keyId</code> is the URL of a remote actor's <code>publicKey</code>. The key is fetched from it with <code>Accept: application/activity+json</code> and cached for a day. A signature that fails against a cached key is checked once more against a fresh one, since the remote may have changed its key.
* Every fetch of a remote document, a key or an actor, is a <code>GET</code> signed as the [[<tvar name="2">Special:MyLanguage/Accounts#Instance actor</tvar>|instance actor]] over <code>(request-target)</code>, <code>host</code> and <code>date</code>, since a server in Mastodon's secure mode refuses unsigned ones. To check it, the remote fetches the instance actor, which is served unsigned, so neither side waits on the other.

<!--T:11-->
The wiki's own key pair is generated the first time it is needed and stored in the database, so nobody has a secret to keep. The public key every actor publishes is derived from the stored private key.
